// @check
// @error `Showable` is a BOUND, not a type — it declares method members, which constrain a type parameter and have no values. Use it as a constraint: `function f<T: Showable>(x: T)`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `render` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: string` / `: boolean` return annotations below are DECLARED rather than
// deleted: a fixture for a type feature should read as the documentation it is,
// and the checker calling them redundant is a fact about inference, not about
// the example. (`@hint` is how a corpus file says "yes, on purpose".)
// A BOUND IN VALUE POSITION IS A DESIGN REFUSAL, not a capability gap, and the
// distinction matters for how it is counted: `{ toString(): string }` as a
// parameter type would be an existential needing dynamic dispatch, and everything
// in VL monomorphizes. `docs/constraints-design.md` §"Explicitly avoided" rules
// out trait objects; the WasmGC-subtyping future is the only revisit trigger.
//
// So a bound alias is registered in its own table and NOT in `cUserTypes` — it has
// no type-arena entry to be spelled with. The sentence names the position it IS
// legal in, because an author who wrote it here almost certainly meant a bound.
type Showable = { toString(): string }

function render(x: Showable): string { "x" }

print("unreached")
